  "body": "## Why\nProject #4's #1145 adapter previously executed only scalar programs. Seven additional owned, structurally verified seeds covering calls, storage, objects and arrays returned unsupported.\n\n## Change\nAdmit a closed managed Int32 profile with at most four static methods, four integer arguments, sixteen local slots per method, four integer statics, one exact four-field FuzzBox layout and integer arrays. Scan every instruction triple, including unreachable code, and close metadata before VM construction.\n\nIncrease the active-frame cap from one to four to execute calls. Keep the 1,024-instruction, 4,096-byte aggregate stack, 8,192-byte managed heap and disposable-process budgets unchanged. Disable frame pooling. Preserve the exact controlled-fault allowlist; RuntimeException and unclassified faults remain findings.\n\nRecord actual execution outcomes, resource observations and disposal. Preserve the first two seed wire hashes and add seven deterministic seeds with finite boundary regressions.\n\n## Validation\n- Independent read-only review found no blocking resource-budget or host-capability gap.\n- Parent `8019f2f9b22e26d7c221454f3b751b0b0e0c67df`: all nine seeds verify; two execute and seven are unsupported.\n- Tested commit `cde74a88fcb812acf3df1a98264f200ecf4397d4`, tree `af118c4c76c711617cbe2a5b84ccf1af5ae440cd`: 39 focused tests pass, zero failures/skips/cancellations.\n- Tests prove positive four-frame calls, fifth-frame refusal, aggregate stack refusal, exact 1,020-element / 8,192-byte array allocation, next-element refusal, metadata and unreachable-opcode exclusion, cancellation and conservative fault handling.\n- The first local launch lacked workspace package links and did not reach test bodies; offline workspace installation resolved setup. Both raw outputs are retained.\n- Published Git tree matches the tested tree exactly. Commit metadata differs because publication uses the GitHub connector.\n\n## Acceptance boundary\nThis advances #1145; it does not close it. General verified-image execution, exception handlers, CIL, native/Rust runtimes and other platforms remain outside this explicitly bounded profile. No nightly or general memory-safety qualification is claimed.\n\nSerial policy: one implementation batch; wait for PR core and main core before the next validation batch.",
